 Operational Status †status.arxiv.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.13316v1] Visual Interestingness Decoded: How GPT-4o Mirrors Human Interests (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13316v1)\nciteturn3048view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13316v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:Visual Interestingness Decoded: How GPT-4o Mirrors Human Interests\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Fitim Abdullahu , cite6†Helmut Grabner L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Visual Interestingness Decoded: How GPT-4o Mirrors Human Interests, by Fitim Abdullahu and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Our daily life is highly influenced by what we consume and see. Attracting and holding one's attention -- the definition of (visual) interestingness -- is essential. The rise of Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) trained on large-scale visual and textual data has demonstrated impressive capabilities.\nL17: We explore these models' potential to understand to what extent the concepts of visual interestingness are captured and examine the alignment between human assessments and GPT-4o's, a leading LMM, predictions through comparative analysis. Our studies reveal partial alignment between humans and GPT-4o. It already captures the concept as best compared to state-of-the-art methods.\nL18: Hence, this allows for the effective labeling of image pairs according to their (commonly) interestingness, which are used as training data to distill the knowledge into a learning-to-rank model. The insights pave the way for a deeper understanding of human interest.\nL19: Comments:  | ICCV 2025\nL20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2510.13316 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2510.13316v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.13316†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Fitim Abdullahu [cite12†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 15 Oct 2025 09:04:48 UTC (2,061 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Visual Interestingness Decoded: How GPT-4o Mirrors Human Interests, by Fitim Abdullahu and 1 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite7†View PDF L36:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite13†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite14†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CV\nL43: \nL44: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L45: \nL46: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite20†cs L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite21†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite24†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Connected Papers (cite25†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Litmaps (ci